# Judge contract

> **Scope change (2026-10-17):** goPekko has no tenancy module. The relations and mutations that mention tenancy or `tenancy.Owner` (P2, T4, T5, M44, M50) specify a requirement that is replaced; they are kept unchanged and are historical, no code implements them. Row ids and classification are not altered.


- Status: proposed, for owner review
- Date: 2026-10-08
- Governs: `migration-kit/prompts/00b-judge-setup.md` (pre-Step 1) and Step 6 (behavior matching)
- Replaces: the parity criterion in `migration/stage-1/feasibility.md` §3.3 and §9 (see its Amendments)

The judge decides parity by checking **observable events against invariants and guaranteed order relations**, never by requiring two runs to produce the same trace. A run passes when every guaranteed relation holds and no unexpected event appears. Two valid interleavings both pass. A missing, extra or misordered guaranteed event fails.

Every guarantee below was checked against `upstream/pekko` at `v1.7.1` (`9a54d12`). Paths are relative to `upstream/pekko/`; `docs/…` means `docs/src/main/paradox/…`.

## 1. Why not identical traces

The feasibility report proposed comparing normalised traces and requiring 100 byte-identical runs. Pekko does not promise a total order, so that criterion fails runs that Pekko itself considers correct:

- Messages from different senders may interleave (`docs/general/message-delivery-reliability.md:119-129`).
- Ordering is not transitive across intermediaries (`…:141-155`).
- A child's failure and its last user messages to the parent may arrive in either order (`…:172-183`, `docs/actors.md:494-498`).

A non-identical trace therefore says nothing about correctness, and normalising traces until they match would hide real defects. The judge asserts relations instead.

## 2. Observable events

Both harnesses record the same event vocabulary through the public API only. Scenario actors are test code in each harness, so they record their own hooks.

| Event | Recorded when | Fields |
|---|---|---|
| `spawned` | spawn returns | actor |
| `pre-start`, `post-stop` | the hook runs | actor, instance |
| `pre-restart`, `post-restart` | the hook runs | actor, instance, cause kind, failed message (pre-restart, when there is one) |
| `sent` | at the `tell` call site | from, to, message |
| `received` | receive starts handling a message | actor, instance, message |
| `decided` | a decider written by the scenario returns | supervisor, child, cause kind, directive |
| `terminated` | a watcher handles `Terminated` | watcher, subject |
| `dead-letter` | an event-stream subscriber gets a dead letter whose message is one of the scenario's message types | message, recipient |
| `ask-replied`, `ask-failed` | the ask completes | asker, target, reply or failure kind |

- **Identity** is the scenario's logical name (`parent`, `child-1`), plus an instance counter per actor that increments on restart. Paths, UIDs, thread names and timestamps are not recorded.
- **No sender field.** ADR 0010 (D2, proposed) gives goPekko no implicit sender, and Pekko replaces a missing sender with `deadLetters` (`actor/src/main/scala/org/apache/pekko/actor/ActorRef.scala:686-690`), so a sender can never match. Reply addresses travel in the message and are compared as payload. If the owner chooses an implicit sender, `sender` returns to `received` and `dead-letter`.
- **Ask failure kinds.** Pekko fails both cases with the same exception class (`actor/…/pattern/AskSupport.scala:312-318`), so the kind comes from the path, not from the exception: `recipient-terminated` when the ask has already failed when the call returns, `timeout` when it fails as the deadline passes, and `rejected` (goPekko only, `ErrRejected`) when a guard rejected the asked message.
- **Default decider.** A supervisor without a scenario decider records no `decided` event; scenarios that rely on the default decider (G7) assert the lifecycle events it produces instead.
- **Ordering.** Each actor's events carry a per-actor sequence number; an actor processes one message at a time, so its own events are totally ordered. A global sequence number exists only for diagnostics and is never asserted.

### Recording

The event log is **not** produced by an observer, and not by any queue that may drop. Observers are asynchronous and lossy by design (ADR 0008, "Observer execution"); a verdict that depends on them could pass because an event was dropped.

- Each harness owns an **append-only recorder**. Scenario code (hooks, handlers, probes) appends synchronously to a **per-actor buffer**, and the append returns an acknowledgement (that actor's sequence number) only after the event is stored. Buffers are merged only at `Flush`, which returns when every append so far is acknowledged; end-of-run checks run after it. There is no lock shared between actors on the append path, so the recorder adds no happens-before edge between actor goroutines that `go test -race` could mistake for synchronization of the runtime under test. The calibration file records which recorder was used.
- **Provenance is observed through a harness guard, never through observers.** The harness registers a recording `Guard` that logs every delivery it is asked to evaluate. A delivery received by the actor and present in the guard log was `Application`; one received and absent from the log was `Runtime`. This is what E10 and E13 assert. `Origin()` read by observers is not used (observers are not evidence).
- **Dead letters.** They reach the recorder through a harness-owned subscriber that is registered before the first send. The recorder keeps **every** dead letter it receives, unfiltered; filtering to the scenario's message types is applied only to the events compared. On goPekko the harness compares the unfiltered count with the monotonic `DeadLettersPublished` counter (a runtime requirement, ADR 0010), read at the scenario-complete boundary after a subscriber barrier (a sentinel the subscriber acknowledges), waiting a bounded time for equality. A gap is a **harness error**, never a pass. **Asymmetry, stated:** Pekko has no such counter, so on the Pekko side completeness rests on the barrier alone (the subscriber registered before the first send, and the sentinel acknowledged after the last).
- `ObserverFault` events and observer statistics are never evidence for a parity verdict. They are checked by their own scenarios (E11, E12), through the statistics, not through the judge log.
- Events keep their source, so a missing source is distinguishable from a missing event.

