Restart windows (`maxNrOfRetries` within a time range) are measured with `System.nanoTime` in Pekko (`actor/…/actor/FaultHandling.scala:66-88`), not with the scheduler. A manual clock cannot drive them, so windowed retry scenarios run only in concurrent mode, with margins wide enough not to depend on timing.

## 6. Proving the judge detects defects

The judge is real only when it fails on broken code.

1. **Self-tests (CI, every PR):** hand-written event logs per family. Every valid alternative interleaving must pass; every invalid one must fail with the expected rule id.
2. **Mutations on the Pekko side (every mutation without the `(goPekko)` mark):** applied to a scratch copy outside the repository, never to `upstream/pekko`. The minimum set is listed below. Each must make at least one scenario fail, and the report lists the failing scenario and rule per mutation.
3. **Mutations on the goPekko side:** every mutation, including those marked `(goPekko)`, which have no Pekko equivalent. Applied once the runtime exists.
4. **No required relation without a mutation.** Every G, I and S relation in section 3 must be broken by at least one mutation above, and every such relation must be asserted by at least one scenario. A relation with no mutation, or with no scenario, is a gap in the contract. The relation to scenario to mutation matrix is in `migration/stage-1/milestone-1.md`, and `scripts/check-judge-matrix.py` fails when a row is missing. The matrix records the **designed** detection. The **observed** one is not typed in any document: 00b writes `judge/mutation-report.tsv`, one row per mutation, scenario and mode, with the run counts and an `outcome` (`scripts/check-judge-matrix.py` reads it and accepts only pairs that were designed). `outcome` is the state of the **campaign**, not the number of violations: `failed` (the judge caught the mutation) needs every requested run to be judgeable and at least one to fail; `survived` needs every requested run to pass; a campaign with a harness error or a timeout among its runs is `harness-error` or `timeout` even if other runs failed (a run counts by the state of its execution, not by what its log shows: a run that ended in timeout is a timeout even when its prefix already proves a violation, which the comparator reports as verdict `fail` with state `timeout`), and a set of logs that is not exactly the runs requested (missing, duplicate or surplus identifiers) is `incomplete`. The violations seen stay in the row (`fail`, `fired_rules`) as **observations**, never as detection or survival. A **clause** is observed when at least one mutation designed to break it was caught by that clause in a valid campaign; a designed mutation that was not caught, not implemented or not a valid result is an unmet expectation, listed and never erasing another mutation's detection. A relation has every clause observed when each of its clauses is observed. Designed coverage, observed detection and execution reliability are reported as three separate facts.

   **The JVM of a Pekko row (`jdk`, `sbt_version`, `platform`).** The Pekko code runs inside the JVM of sbt (the `judge` project does not fork `run`), and `sdkman-init.sh`, which the runners source, replaces `PATH` and `JAVA_HOME`; `JAVA_8_HOME` alone only feeds Pekko's build. So `judge/run-pekko.sh` and `judge/mutate.py run` pass `-java-home "$JAVA_8_HOME"` to sbt, read the `welcome to sbt` banner and refuse to register a row (or exit 3) when the Java is not 1.8 (`judge/jdk.py`, `judge/selftest_jdk.py`). The three columns record what the banner said; A refusal aborts the whole `run` call (the rows of earlier mutations of that call are not written; their `sbt.log` files stay in the work directory), so run one mutation per call, as the campaign driver does. `-` marks a row that no run of this tree produced (re-evaluation, not implemented) or an earlier row whose runner did not record them. The 13 rows of the campaign of #1489 carry `11.0.32`: they ran on JDK 11 (correction of 2026-11-09 in `migration/stage-1/judge-pekko-1484/PROVENANCE.md`).

   **Pekko baselines (`judge/pekko-baseline-report.tsv`, `judge/baseline.py`).** A mutation row says the judge catches a defect; a baseline row says the same scenario passes on unmutated Pekko. `baseline.py run <copy> <workdir> <scenarios>` runs each scenario in every mode it declares (S21 only concurrent; 30 concurrent runs, 1 controlled) with the same preflight, classification and JVM rule as `mutate.py run`, and writes one row per scenario and mode (`passed`, `failed`, `harness-error`, `timeout`, `incomplete`, `compile-error`; columns in the docstring). A baseline row is CURRENT by the rule of the mutation rows (`pekko_current.py`). `scripts/check-judge-matrix.py` prints `Pekko baseline CURRENT` and `Pekko V3 evidence`: a designed detection is listed only when its mutation row is CURRENT and ran on Java 1.8, the baseline of its scenario is CURRENT, passed and ran on Java 1.8 in **every** mode the scenario declares, and both rows were produced by the same judge inputs (`pekko_current.same_inputs`: the same commits, or identical files at both). That is the evidence part of level V3; assigning V3 stays an Owner decision. `judge/selftest_baseline.py` tests it.

